<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kolkata</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Calcutta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kolkata (Calcutta) is the capital of the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooghly-river">Hooghly River</a>. It grew from villages where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> set up a trading settlement in 1690, was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> until 1911 and was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. It remains one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s largest cities and a major port and cultural centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kolkata.jpg" alt="Kolkata" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NalGup20 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_maidan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English-language education, newspapers and universities founded in 1857 at <b>Calcutta</b>, Bombay and Madras created a new professional middle class.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet Rajputana was also the homeland of the Marwari merchant communities, whose firms, such as those of the Birla and Bajaj families, had become leading industrial houses in <b>Calcutta</b>, Bombay and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1786 the British judge William Jones told the Asiatic Society in <b>Calcutta</b> that Sanskrit, Greek and Latin were so alike that they must have sprung from a common source, “which, perhaps, no longer exists”: a founding moment of comparative linguistics.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Gandhi took no part in the independence celebrations in Delhi: he spent 15 August 1947 in <b>Calcutta</b>, fasting, praying and spinning in a house in a riot-torn district that he shared with the Muslim League leader Huseyn Shaheed Suhrawardy.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Four printed versions, all of the 19th century, became the basis for translations: <b>Calcutta</b> I, published by the British East India Company in 1814–1818; Bulaq, printed by the Egyptian government in 1835; Breslau, prepared by Christian Maximilian Habicht with Mordecai ibn al-Najjar from 1825, whose “Tunisian manuscript” was…</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Printed editions began with the first volume of Mathew Lumsden's, published in <b>Kolkata</b> in 1811.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
